[0.000, "o", " | kubectl apply -f -\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[27X\r\n  deployment.apps/svc configured\u001b[71X\r\n  deployment.apps/worker created\u001b[71X\r\n  triggerauthentication.keda.sh/sqs created\u001b[60X\r\n  scaledobject.keda.sh/worker created\u001b[66X\u001b[32;103H\u001b[1K\r\n  \u001b[37mShe said it out loud three times in the first incident. This time she writes it down.\u001b[39m\u001b[16X\u001b[34;103H\u001b[1K\r\n  \u001b[32m\u001b[1m(^_^) Madina's notes\u001b(B\u001b[m \u001b[1m. HOW A READINESS FAILURE ACTUALLY REMOVES A POD\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[32X\r\n  \u001b[2m|\u001b(B\u001b[m The Service does not route to pods. It routes to an EndpointSlice,\u001b[33X\r\n  \u001b[2m|\u001b(B\u001b[m and readiness is what puts an address in it or takes it out.\u001b[39X\r\n  \u001b[2m|\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[100X\r\n  \u001b[2m|\u001b(B\u001b[m One pod NotReady   -> its address is removed, the rest carry the load.\u001b[29X\r\n  \u001b[2m|\u001b(B\u001b[m Every pod NotReady -> the slice is empty and the Service has nowhere\u001b[31X\r\n  \u001b[2m|\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[23X\u001b[23Cto send anything. No error, no traffic, no pods.\u001b[29X\r\n  \u001b[2m|\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[100X\r\n  \u001b[2m|\u001b(B\u001b[m So the blast radius of a readiness probe is not one replica.\u001b[39X\u001b[44;103H\u001b[1K\u001b[?12l\u001b[?25h\r\u001b[?2026l"]
